concept("load-building", "de", "Load building and consolidation", M, "I",
  "load building consolidation optimisation truckload ftl ptl ltl utilisation weight cube volume pallet multi-stop vehicle fill",
  "Load building groups shipments onto vehicles to fill capacity and cut the cost per unit, while respecting delivery windows and vehicle limits.",
  "Consolidation combines shipments travelling the same way into one truckload instead of several part-loads. The planner, or the TMS optimiser, balances weight and volume (cube) limits, equipment type, delivery time windows, stop sequence, and compatibility rules such as not mixing food with chemicals. Multi-stop loads save money but add stops and delay risk; pooling and cross-docking are related strategies. The data needed is shipment weight, volume, pallet count, ready and due dates, and lane. Typical metrics are weight and cube utilisation, cost per kg and stops per load.",
  "Five part-load shipments of about 3 tonnes each from a Chennai plant to Bengaluru dealers are consolidated onto one 16-tonne truck with four stops, cutting the cost per kg by about 35%.",
  "A truck is at 95% of its weight limit but only 50% of its volume. What does that tell you about the freight?",
  "Expect questions on what limits a load (weight, cube, time windows), how to measure utilisation, and the trade-offs of multi-stop consolidation.",
  ["tms-data-model", "freight-rating", "route-optimisation"]),
concept("edi-carriers", "de", "Carrier connectivity and EDI (204, 990, 214, 210)", M, "I",
  "edi x12 204 990 214 210 997 tender acceptance shipment status invoice carrier connectivity edifact as2 van integration",
  "Shippers and carriers exchange tenders, acceptances, tracking updates and invoices as standard EDI messages, increasingly alongside APIs.",
  "In the ANSI X12 standard, widely used in North America, the 204 is the motor carrier load tender, the 990 is the carrier's accept or decline response, the 214 carries shipment status updates such as pickup, in transit and delivered, and the 210 is the freight invoice. A 997 functional acknowledgement confirms that a message was received and parsed. Europe and much of Asia use EDIFACT equivalents such as IFTMIN and IFTSTA, and many Indian carriers connect through APIs, portals or files instead. Messages travel over VANs or AS2 connections and are mapped into the TMS. Data engineers handle parsing, status-code mapping, duplicates and late messages.",
  "The TMS sends a 204 tender for load L-1001. The carrier replies with a 990 accept within 30 minutes, sends 214 updates at pickup and delivery, and later bills with a 210 that the TMS audits against the agreed rate.",
  "A carrier sends the same 214 delivery message twice, and another one out of order. How should your pipeline handle it?",
  "Logistics interviews expect you to know what the 204, 990, 214 and 210 are, the order they flow in, and the data quality problems each one brings.",
  ["shipment-visibility", "freight-settlement", "file-ingestion"]),
concept("shipment-visibility", "de", "Shipment visibility and tracking events", M, "I",
  "shipment visibility tracking events gps telematics eta status in transit delivered exception dwell real time track and trace",
  "Visibility means knowing where every shipment is and when it will arrive, built from a stream of tracking events from carriers, GPS devices and visibility platforms.",
  "Events arrive from EDI 214s, carrier APIs, telematics and GPS pings, driver apps and visibility providers. Each must be mapped to a standard status (picked up, in transit, arrived, delivered, exception), deduplicated, and ordered by event time rather than arrival time, because messages arrive late and out of order. From the event history you derive the current status, actual pickup and delivery times, dwell time at stops, and a predicted ETA. Exceptions such as delays and missed appointments trigger alerts. Coverage, the share of loads with timely tracking, is a key quality measure.",
  "A load's GPS pings stop for three hours near Krishnagiri. The visibility pipeline flags a possible breakdown, the ETA model moves arrival from 18:00 to 21:30, and the customer is notified automatically.",
  "Why should the current status be derived by event time rather than the time the message arrived?",
  "Expect design questions on tracking pipelines: standardising events, late and duplicate events, ETA prediction and alerting.",
  ["edi-carriers", "control-tower", "batch-stream"]),
concept("freight-rating", "de", "Freight rating and tariffs", M, "I",
  "freight rating rate tariff lane rate per km slab accessorial fuel surcharge detention minimum charge contract rate cost ltl ftl parcel",
  "Freight rating calculates what a shipment or load should cost from contracted rates, tariffs, accessorial charges and fuel surcharges.",
  "Rates depend on the mode and the agreement. Full truckload is usually priced per trip or per km on a lane; part-load and LTL by weight, distance band and freight class; parcel by weight slab and zone. On top come accessorials such as detention, loading, extra stops, tolls and out-of-delivery-area charges, plus a fuel surcharge that moves with diesel prices. Minimum charges, rate validity dates and currency all matter. The TMS rates loads before tendering to choose the cheapest suitable carrier, and the same rate engine checks invoices later. Rate tables change over time, so keep effective dates.",
  "A lane rate of ₹28 per km for a 32-foot truck from Pune to Hyderabad over 560 km gives ₹15,680, plus ₹1,200 for loading, plus a 9% fuel surcharge on that total: an expected cost of about ₹18,400.",
  "Why must rate tables keep effective-from and effective-to dates?",
  "Expect questions on rate structures (lane, slab, per km), accessorials and fuel surcharges, and how you'd model rate tables with history.",
  ["freight-settlement", "tms-data-model", "scd"]),
concept("freight-settlement", "de", "Freight audit and settlement", M, "A",
  "freight audit settlement invoice carrier payment rerate tolerance dispute accessorial validation cost allocation accrual self billing 210 gst",
  "Freight audit checks every carrier invoice against what was agreed and what actually happened, before settlement pays the carrier and allocates the cost.",
  "Audit matches each invoice (an EDI 210, an e-invoice or a PDF) to its load, rerates the load with the contracted rates, and validates accessorials against evidence such as gate timestamps for detention or proof of delivery. It catches duplicate invoices, wrong rates, unapproved charges and bills for cancelled loads. Invoices within tolerance are approved; the rest are disputed. Settlement then pays the carrier, sometimes by self-billing without waiting for an invoice, and accrues and allocates cost to orders, products and cost centres for margin reporting. In India, the GST treatment of freight, including reverse charge for some transport services, also has to be right.",
  "A carrier bills ₹21,500 for a load rated at ₹18,400. Audit finds a ₹2,000 detention charge with no gate timestamps and a duplicated loading fee, approves ₹18,400 and disputes the rest.",
  "Why does audit rerate the load instead of trusting the amount on the invoice?",
  "Senior logistics data roles ask how you'd design audit rules, tolerance thresholds, the dispute workflow, and cost allocation to orders.",
  ["freight-rating", "edi-carriers", "data-quality"]),
concept("warehouse-data", "de", "Warehouse operations data: receiving, picking, slotting", M, "I",
  "warehouse wms receiving putaway picking packing dispatch slotting asn 856 wave pick rate lines per hour dock to stock cycle count location",
  "A WMS records every movement inside a warehouse, from receiving and putaway to picking, packing and dispatch, and that data drives productivity and accuracy analytics.",
  "Inbound, advance shipping notices (ASNs, EDI 856) tell the warehouse what's coming, and receipts are checked and put away into locations. Outbound, orders are released in waves or batches, pickers follow pick lists, and items are packed and loaded. Every task has timestamps, a user, a location and a quantity, so you can measure lines picked per hour, pick accuracy, dock-to-stock time and order cycle time. Slotting places fast-moving SKUs near packing to cut travel. Inventory is held at location level, and cycle counts reconcile system stock with physical stock.",
  "Analysis of a Mumbai DC's pick tasks shows 20% of SKUs account for 80% of picks. Re-slotting those SKUs into golden-zone locations near packing raises picking from 95 to 120 lines per hour.",
  "Which timestamps would you need to measure dock-to-stock time?",
  "Expect questions on WMS data grains (task, order line, location), productivity metrics, and slotting analysis with ABC classification.",
  ["inventory-analytics", "sc-landscape", "logistics-kpis"]),
concept("inventory-analytics", "de", "Inventory and replenishment analytics", M, "I",
  "inventory stock replenishment safety stock reorder point turns days of supply fill rate stockout abc analysis service level snapshot semi-additive",
  "Inventory analytics balances having enough stock to meet demand against the cost of holding too much, using safety stock, reorder points and turnover measures.",
  "Key measures are stock on hand, inventory turns (cost of goods sold divided by average inventory), days of supply, fill rate and stock-outs. The reorder point is expected demand during the lead time plus safety stock, and safety stock grows with demand variability, lead time variability and the target service level. ABC analysis focuses attention on the few SKUs that drive most of the value, and XYZ analysis adds demand variability. The data needed is daily stock snapshots by SKU and location, demand history, lead times and open purchase orders. Snapshots can be summed across products but not across days.",
  "A SKU sells 40 units a day with a 5-day lead time and 60 units of safety stock, so its reorder point is 40 × 5 + 60 = 260 units.",
  "Why can't you sum daily stock snapshots across a month to get total inventory?",
  "Expect the formulas (reorder point, turns, days of supply), ABC analysis, and modelling inventory snapshots as semi-additive facts.",
  ["warehouse-data", "forecasting", "star-schema"]),
concept("logistics-kpis", "de", "Logistics KPIs: OTIF, cost per shipment and dwell time", M, "F",
  "logistics kpi otif on time in full transit time cost per shipment cost per kg dwell detention tender acceptance carrier scorecard",
  "Logistics performance is tracked with a few KPIs: on-time in-full delivery, cost per unit shipped, transit time, and how long vehicles wait at facilities.",
  "OTIF counts orders delivered both on time, within the agreed window, and in full, with the complete quantity. The definition must state the window, which date counts (requested or promised) and the grain (order or line). Cost measures include freight cost per shipment, per kg and as a percentage of sales. Transit time runs from pickup to delivery; dwell or detention time is the wait at a pickup or delivery location beyond the free time. Carrier scorecards add tender acceptance rate and tracking coverage. Write a definition for every KPI, because arguments about what counts as on time waste more time than bad data.",
  "Of 1,200 deliveries in October, 1,104 were on time and 1,150 were in full, but only 1,068 were both, so OTIF is 89%: lower than the on-time rate (92%) or the in-full rate (95.8%).",
  "Why is OTIF lower than both the on-time rate and the in-full rate?",
  "Interviewers ask you to define OTIF precisely, choose its grain, and explain how you'd build a carrier scorecard.",
  ["kpi-metrics", "shipment-visibility", "freight-rating"]),
concept("route-optimisation", "de", "Route optimisation and last-mile delivery", M, "A",
  "route optimisation vehicle routing problem vrp travelling salesman tsp last mile time windows geocoding distance matrix or-tools heuristics delivery",
  "Route optimisation decides which vehicle visits which stops and in what order, to minimise distance and cost while meeting time windows and capacity limits.",
  "This is the vehicle routing problem (VRP), an extension of the travelling salesman problem with several vehicles, capacity limits and delivery time windows. Exact solutions are only practical for small cases, so solvers such as Google OR-Tools use heuristics and metaheuristics. The inputs are geocoded addresses, a distance or travel-time matrix, service time at each stop, vehicle capacities and shift limits. Last-mile delivery adds failed-delivery risk, cash on delivery, and traffic that changes by the hour; in Indian cities, geocoding free-text addresses is often the biggest problem. Compare planned and actual routes to improve the inputs.",
  "A Bengaluru grocery hub routes 180 drops across 12 vans with two-hour delivery slots. Optimised routes cut total distance by 18% and late deliveries by half compared with zone-based manual planning.",
  "Why does poor address geocoding hurt route optimisation more than a weaker solver does?",
  "Expect to explain VRP constraints, why heuristics are used, and the data you'd need, including how you'd measure plan against actual.",
  ["load-building", "logistics-kpis", "capacity-planning"]),
concept("capacity-planning", "de", "Demand and capacity planning", M, "A",
  "capacity planning demand planning forecast lanes trucks drivers docks labour peak season diwali spot market scenario planning",
  "Capacity planning matches forecast freight volumes with the trucks, drivers, docks and warehouse labour needed to move them, before the peak arrives.",
  "It starts from a demand forecast by lane, week and mode, converted into the capacity needed: truckloads, vehicle types, dock slots and labour hours. Compare that with committed carrier capacity and contracts, and plan for the gaps with spot market buying, temporary labour or pre-building inventory. In India, festive peaks such as Diwali, harvest seasons and monsoon disruption shape both demand and transit times. Scenario planning (best, base and worst case) and tracking forecast accuracy by lane keep the plan honest.",
  "The forecast shows Delhi to Lucknow volume rising from 40 to 70 truckloads a week in the three weeks before Diwali, so the team contracts 20 extra trucks and plans to buy the rest on the spot market.",
  "Why plan capacity by lane and week instead of using one national monthly forecast?",
  "Senior roles ask how you'd turn a demand forecast into capacity needs, and how you'd handle peaks and forecast error.",
  ["forecasting", "route-optimisation", "logistics-kpis"]),
concept("control-tower", "de", "Supply chain control tower analytics", M, "A",
  "control tower visibility exceptions alerts end to end orders shipments inventory dashboard predictive delays resolution canonical model",
  "A control tower is one place where teams see orders, inventory and shipments end to end, get alerted to exceptions, and act before customers notice.",
  "It combines data from the ERP, OMS, WMS, TMS, carriers and visibility platforms into a shared model of orders, shipments and events. Rules and models detect exceptions such as late pickups, stock-outs, missed appointments and predicted delays, and route them to the right team with a recommended action. Good control towers rank exceptions by business impact rather than by count, and track how quickly each one is resolved. Underneath, the data platform needs near-real-time ingestion, a canonical shipment model and reliable identifiers across systems.",
  "The control tower flags 14 shipments for a key customer that are at risk of missing tomorrow's delivery window, ranked by order value, and the transport team moves 3 loads to a faster carrier.",
  "Why rank exceptions by business impact rather than by count?",
  "Architecture interviews ask what data and integrations a control tower needs, how you'd model exceptions, and how you'd avoid alert fatigue.",
  ["shipment-visibility", "logistics-kpis", "sc-landscape"]),
